@page "/"
@page "/{*shellPath}"

@*
    The Explorer's routable shell surface, and the one place the shell's route
    model is bound to the browser.

    Two templates, both landing here. "/" is the bare address, which means
    "restore where I was"; the catch-all takes every addressable view the shell
    can show - /explore/trees/orders/data, /tenants?tenant=acme, and anything a
    future area contributes - without this file having to enumerate them. The
    literal /not-found route still wins over the catch-all, because Blazor
    matches a literal segment ahead of a catch-all.

    Deliberately a catch-all rather than a fixed set of templates: an address is
    parsed here by ExplorerRoutePath, not by Blazor route-parameter binding,
    because a selection id is cluster-owned and may contain a slash (a
    tenant-owned tree is t/acme/orders). Escaping it and parsing it ourselves is
    what keeps such an id addressable at all. It also means an unrecognised
    address renders the shell rather than a dead end, which is the graceful
    degradation a stale bookmark is owed.

    The markup is unchanged: the chrome around it belongs to the layout and the
    app shell, and this page contributes only the detail surface it always did.
*@

<PageTitle>@ExplorerInfo.DisplayName</PageTitle>

<DetailPanel />
